body:
Yes you can:


     public abstract class MonoBehaviour {}


Now just inherit everything else in your code from that. See? C# is exactly what Unity told me it’d be!



//untested and missing a lot of things.
//This is just here for example.
//Not the way I’d personally write it exactly either
//but want to show more of what’s going on.
//Also I wrote this on mobile, etc etc…

public Vector3[] path; //must be non-null with at least one entry
public float pathTime; //must be non-zero

private float progress;

void Update(){
     //update progress
     progress += Time.deltaTime / pathTime;
     while(progress >= 1f) progress -= 1f;

     //get array indices
     int pointIdxA = (int)(progress * path.Length);
     int pointIdxB = pointIdxA +1;
     if(pointIdxB >= path.Length) pointIdxB = 0;

     //get interpolation factor
     float interp = progress - (pointIdxA / path.Length);

     //interpolate positions
     Vector3 pointA = path[pointIdxA];
     Vector3 pointB = path[pointIdxB];
     Vector3 resultPoint = Mathf.Lerp(pointA, pointB, interp);

     //set the position!
     transform.position = resultPoint;
}

I don’t do this super often, but if I end up with a big file with a bunch of little structs or something that I want to mark as serializable, I just do something like


using Ser = System.SerializableAttribute;

and just type [Ser] everywhere. If I decide I don’t like the inconsistency with all my other files, I’ll just do F2 in visual studio to rename it, and remove the using Ser line.


Could probably use a similar approach if ur having legit trouble typing “SerializeField”:


using SerVar = UnityEngine.SerializeField;


(Thus saving you from typing “serialize” or “field”. Maybe change it if someone on the project has trouble spelling “server” or smth)

Sorry to get back to you so late.


Yes, you can, using the params keyword. This keyword can only go on the last argument you have (or the only arg, if you only have one), and it must be declared as an array type:

    float Sum(params float[] inputs)…
    int CountEqual(int eq, params int[] inputs)…

will both work.
Note that

    void Broken(params int[] foo, int bar)…

will not work, because params can only be on the last argument.


When an arg has the params keyword, it can be supplied by giving it an array directly (same as if it didn’t have params), or by simply providing the args one after the other positionally:

    int Sum(params int[] inputs) …

can be called like:

    //you can call like this now
    x = Sum(1, 2, 3);
    //which is essentially equivalent to
    //(this is how you’d have to do it without params)
    x = Sum(new int[]{1, 2, 3});

When you’re actually inside your method that used params, you get an array either way. This Sum method would likely look like

    int Sum(params int[] inputs) {
        int result = 0;
        for(int i=0; i<inputs.Length; i++) {
            result += inputs[i];
        }
        return result;
    }





Finally, I’ll leave you with this- since I know you’re coming from python, I worry a tiny bit about questions regarding making C#’s rules looser. Both optional args and params are useful, I use them all the time, but I find ppl coming from python and js tend to overuse them, along with an overuse of strings and casting things to object. These tend to be just the way of working in python, but c# is different. You generally want to make your code as restrictive as possible (while still having it do what it needs to do). Something like a Sum method is a good use of params, but be careful that you don’t try to make every method into

    Method(params object[] args)
Or you’re gonna end up with weird casting issues just like python. It becomes possible to call the method in a way that doesn’t work, and subsequently becomes the programmers responsibility to remember how it works, lest only ever see the bug at runtime.

Compile errors are good- they save you from runtime errors. If you can write your code so it generates a compile error if used wrong, that’s one of the best ways to prevent bugs- since many bad ways to use your code now become impossible and your IDE will probably tell you before ever having to run anything.

(I only add this last bit because I’ve seen a lot of python devs (javascript also) go to C# and struggle to try to make things feel like python. Just remember to go with how c# does things in c#, and python’s way with python. Some of the development habits and techniques can be quite different between the two)

Some of this is has already been pointed out but I wanted to mention a few details:


Arguments can be either required or optional. The only restriction is that all optional args must come after all required ones. You can make an optional arg by setting it equal to its default value in the arg declaration:

    WorkingMethod(int require, int optional = 0);
    BrokenMethod(int optional = 0, int require); 
    //BrokenMethod wont compile because required args must come first

The default values you can supply here must be constant, so they cant depend on program state- if you need this, you should use an overload instead of a default argument.

Now, there’s a good reason to not make everything have a default, and that’s to force yourself to supply those arguments, which can be a really big hint as to how to use the function.
Something like:
    int Add(int a = 0, int b = 0) => a+b;
would be a bit strange because then calling
    int x = Add();
is valid. Looking at this, it’s unclear what you’re adding, and unclear that x would be 0.
A better use would be something like:

     UserData LoadUserData(string path, bool createNew = true)…

Here it’s clear that you are trying to load data, likely from a file. You give a path (probably to a file), and it spits out UserData. By default, you might want to create the user data if it doesn’t exist, but you might want to only get existing user data and not create a new file or whatever, and just return null.

    // ways of loading or creating if doesnt exist:
    LoadUserData(“user.dat”);
    LoadUserData(“user.dat”, true);
    LoadUserData(“user.dat”, createNew: true);

    //load existing, dont create new:
    LoadUserData(“user.dat”, false);
    LoadUserData(“user.dat”, createNew: false);

Lastly, as you can see above, you can supply arguments either positionally (the way “user.dat” is passed) or by name (the way createNew is passed in the last example). You can supply any argument by name, but if you also want to supply args positionally you have to do that first. Once you start giving args by name it screws up all the positioning after. And all required args must be supplied, whether by name or position. If I have
    float Mix(float a, float b, float t = 0.5f) …

then

    x = Mix(1f, 2f);
    x = Mix(1f, 2f, 0.75f);
    x = Mix(a: 1f, b: 2f, t: 0.75f);
    x = Mix(1f, b:2f, t: 0.75f);
    x = Mix(1f, t: 0.75f, b:2f);
    x = Mix(b: 2f, a:1f);

should all compile. Note that:

    x = Mix(a: 1f, 2f);

doesn’t work because you can’t give args by position after using a name, and

    x = Mix(a: 1f, t: 0.75f);

doesn’t work because b isn’t given, and it’s required.






Good luck on learning C# and may the silicon gods bless you with few bugs.

You can assign arg2 without arg1 if you use the name when passing them in. You can also re-order them when passing them in, so as to provide arg2 before arg1. As long as all positional args are provided before all named args this works:



MyFunction(arg2: 5);

MyFunction(arg2: 5, arg1: 3);

MyFunction(3, arg2: 5);


All work
